{"id":"twitter:1998041983630385647","tweet_id":"1998041983630385647","source":"twitter","type":"replies","url":"/archive/twitter/?id=1998041983630385647","title":"@WadeMiller but this is where Eastman's distinction is dubious, especially by ending birth right citizenship by Executive Order At the heart of Schuck","summary":"@WadeMiller but this is where Eastman's distinction is dubious, especially by ending birth right citizenship by Executive Order At the heart of Schuck and Smith's argument is rejection of Common Law idea of the monarch deciding citizenship \"partial territorial jurisdiction\" is made up","date":"2025-12-08","published":"2025-12-08T14:48:25+00:00","author":"Greg McVerry","reply_to":"https://twitter.com/WadeMiller/status/1998039637781368894","reply_tweet_id":"1998039637781368894","tags":[],"body_html":"<p>@WadeMiller but this is where Eastman&#x27;s distinction is dubious, especially by ending birth right citizenship by Executive Order<br><br>At the heart of Schuck and Smith&#x27;s argument is rejection of Common Law idea of the monarch deciding citizenship<br><br>&quot;partial territorial jurisdiction&quot; is made up</p>","original_url":"https://twitter.com/jgmac1106/status/1998041983630385647","display_date":"December 08, 2025 · 14:48 UTC","access":"PUBLIC","publish_status":"published","is_retweet":false,"photos":[]}
{"id":"twitter:1998039762301854051","tweet_id":"1998039762301854051","source":"twitter","type":"replies","url":"/archive/twitter/?id=1998039762301854051","title":"@WadeMiller Further we know in Slaughter-House Cases (1873) that Courts applied to restriction exclusively to children of foreign ministers We know fr","summary":"@WadeMiller Further we know in Slaughter-House Cases (1873) that Courts applied to restriction exclusively to children of foreign ministers We know from United States v. Wong Kim Ark (1898) that the Courts applied the concept of common law, and this was reaffirmed in Plyler v. Doe (1982)","date":"2025-12-08","published":"2025-12-08T14:39:35+00:00","author":"Greg McVerry","reply_to":"https://twitter.com/jgmac1106/status/1998039078252736579","reply_tweet_id":"1998039078252736579","tags":[],"body_html":"<p>@WadeMiller Further we know in Slaughter-House Cases (1873) that Courts applied to restriction exclusively to children of foreign ministers<br><br>We know from United States v. Wong Kim Ark (1898) that the Courts applied the concept of common law, and this was reaffirmed in Plyler v. Doe (1982)</p>","original_url":"https://twitter.com/jgmac1106/status/1998039762301854051","display_date":"December 08, 2025 · 14:39 UTC","access":"PUBLIC","publish_status":"published","is_retweet":false,"photos":[]}
{"id":"twitter:1998039078252736579","tweet_id":"1998039078252736579","source":"twitter","type":"replies","url":"/archive/twitter/?id=1998039078252736579","title":"@WadeMiller Anyone who has followed 14th amendment debate knows how Eastman has tried to use phrase. But the debates of Trumbull and Howard in 1866 ne","summary":"@WadeMiller Anyone who has followed 14th amendment debate knows how Eastman has tried to use phrase. But the debates of Trumbull and Howard in 1866 never mention \"partial territorial jurisdiction\" Elk v. Wilkins in 1884 never mentions \"partial territorial jurisdiction\" Eastman made it up","date":"2025-12-08","published":"2025-12-08T14:36:52+00:00","author":"Greg McVerry","reply_to":"https://twitter.com/jgmac1106/status/1998035244432396609","reply_tweet_id":"1998035244432396609","tags":[],"body_html":"<p>@WadeMiller Anyone who has followed 14th amendment debate knows how Eastman has tried to use phrase.<br><br>But the debates of Trumbull and Howard in 1866 never mention &quot;partial territorial jurisdiction&quot;<br><br>Elk v. Wilkins in 1884 never mentions &quot;partial territorial jurisdiction&quot;<br><br>Eastman made it up</p>","original_url":"https://twitter.com/jgmac1106/status/1998039078252736579","display_date":"December 08, 2025 · 14:36 UTC","access":"PUBLIC","publish_status":"published","is_retweet":false,"photos":[]}
